On Julia 1.12 a wide argument tuple can leave a call to `ijl_apply_generic` during derivative compilation and raise an unexpected-calling-convention error. The following case puts an active reference at the end of 32 constant vectors.

```julia
using Enzyme
@inline wide_read(r::Base.RefValue) = r[]
@inline wide_read(v::AbstractVector) = sum(v)
@inline wide_sum(::Tuple{}) = 0.0
@inline wide_sum(xs::Tuple) = wide_read(first(xs)) + wide_sum(Base.tail(xs))
@noinline inner(x, xs::Vararg{Any,N}) where N = wide_sum(xs) * x[1]
function outer(x, xs::Vararg{Any,N}) where N
    last(xs)[] = x[2]
    return inner(x, xs...)
end
vs = ntuple(j -> fill(Float64(j), 3), 32)
x, dx = [0.3, 0.7], zeros(2)
autodiff(ReverseWithPrimal, outer, Active, Duplicated(x, dx),
         map(Const, vs)..., Duplicated(Ref(0.0), Ref(0.0)))
@assert dx ≈ [3 * sum(1:32) + x[2], x[1]]
```

The same case with 31 vectors works. Forward mode and placing the reference first exercise the same boundary. PR #3994 supplies the inference change. The focused regression checks the gradients and the mutation in both positions.

A function with a valid numeric branch and an error branch returns its normal result when the error branch is skipped. Its derivative should behave the same way.

```julia
using Enzyme
@noinline invalid_type(::Type{T}) where {T} = Complex{String}
function f(x, take)
    if take
        invalid_type(typeof(x))
        return x
    end
    return 2x
end
@assert f(1.5, false) == 3.0
@assert autodiff(Reverse, f, Active, Active(1.5), Const(false))[1][1] == 2.0
```

On the affected compiler path, forming `Complex{String}` raises a type error while compiling the derivative even when `take` is false. The invalid branch should still throw when `take` is true. The regression checks reverse mode, forward mode and runtime activity.
